>> title: Lotus Root and Dried Octopus Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, lotus root, dried octopus, peanuts, cantonese, old-fire soup, autumn, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 蓮藕章魚豬骨湯, lin ngau zoeng jyu zyu gwat tong, lin ngau jeung yu jue gwat tong, lotus root and dried octopus soup, lotus root pork bone soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 45 min
>> slack: forgiving — lotus root cut into thin coins falls apart into the broth and clouds it, and thick chunks are what hold up
>> step.1: Autumn into winter. The dried octopus is the whole reason this tastes of more than lotus root — it is the savour, not a garnish, and a pot without it is a different soup.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the bones
>> step.3: soak the octopus 20 min, rinse the rest
>> step.4: simmer 3 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

Autumn into winter. The dried octopus is the whole reason this tastes of more than lotus root — it is the savour, not a garnish, and a pot without it is a different soup.

Blanch @pork neck bones{2%lb}(900 g) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse every bone under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Soak @dried octopus{1%small}(zoeng jyu gon; one is enough for a whole pot) in warm water, ~soak{20%min}, then cut it up and rinse @raw peanuts{1/2%cup}(faa sang; raw, not roasted, and they go in dry), @black-eyed peas{1/3%cup}(mei dau; the spring-damp pair with the peanuts) and @red dates{4}(hung zou; pitted — the stone is held to make a pot 燥) alongside it.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched bones{} with @&(~1)octopus and pulses{}, @lotus root{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; in thick chunks, never thin coins), @ginger{3%slices} and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{3%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
